.Dd September 19, 2025
.Dt BASE64_DECODE 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm base64_decode
.Nd decode Base64
.Sh LIBRARY
.Lb libbasse
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In base64.h
.Vt struct base64_decoder ;
.Ft "bool"
.Fo base64_decode
.Fa "struct base64_decoder *restrict ctx" "const char *restrict in" "size_t inlen"
.Fa "char *restrict out" "size_t *restrict outlen"
.Fc
.Ft "size_t"
.Fn base64_decoder_size
.Fd #define BASE64_MAX_DECODER_SIZE 16384
.Ft "void"
.Fn base64_decoder_init "struct base64_decoder *ctx"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Fn base64_decode
function decodes
.Fa inlen
bytes of Base64 data, as defined by RFC 4648, from buffer
.Fa in
into buffer
.Fa out .
.Fa *outlen ,
at the time of call, indicates the maximum number of bytes that can be
written to buffer
.Fa out .
If buffer
.Fa out
does not have enough space to fully decode buffer
.Fa in ,
the
.Fn base64_decode
function will decode as much data as possible, truncating its output to
the nearest multiple of 3 bytes rounded down.
.Pp
If
.Fa ctx
is not
.Dv NULL ,
The
.Fn base64_decode
function ignores newlines
.Ql ( \en
and
.Ql \er )
in buffer
.Fa in .
In addition, if buffer
.Fa in
cannot be fully decoded because the number of characters, sans newlines,
in buffer
.Fa in
is not divisible by 4, the undecoded characters will be stored in
.Fa ctx .
Calling the
.Fn base64_decode
function again with further data in this case allows these characters to
be decoded along with the new data.
.Pp
If
.Fa ctx
is
.Dv NULL ,
the
.Fn base64_decode
function decodes buffer
.Fa in
as a single unit. In such case, the
.Fn base64_decode
function treats newlines as illegal characters, and also fails when the
length of
.Fa in
is not a multiple of 4.
.Pp
The
.Fn base64_decoder_size
function obtains the size of a
.Vt struct base64_decoder
object in bytes, for use with memory allocation routines such as
.Xr malloc 3 .
.Pp
The
.Vt BASE64_MAX_DECODER_SIZE
macro indicates the maximum size of a
.Vt struct base64_decoder .
It is also the largest value that is possibly returned by the
.Fn base64_decoder_size
function. This is intended to be used in environments where
.Xr malloc 3
is unavailable or is discouraged from use.
.Pp
The
.Fn base64_decoder_init
function initializes buffer
.Fa ctx
for use with the
.Fn base64_decode
function. The size of buffer
.Fa ctx ,
in bytes, must not be smaller than
.Vt BASE64_MAX_DECODER_SIZE
for static allocations, or the return value of
.Fn base64_decoder_size
otherwise, and must satisfy the alignment requirement of
.Vt size_t .
.Sh RETURN VALUES
The
.Fn base64_decode
function returns
.Dv true
if no illegal characters are detected in the buffer
.Fa in .
Otherwise, it returns
.Dv false .
The number of bytes written to buffer
.Fa out
is stored in
.Fa *outlen ,
regardless of whether the
.Fn base64_decode
function succeeds.
.Pp
The
.Fn base64_decoder_size
function returns the size in bytes required by a
.Vt struct base64_decoder
object.
.Pp
The
.Fn base64_decoder_init
function does not return a value.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The following program, when compiled and executed, writes
.Ql hello, world
to
.Va stdout .
.Bd -literal -offset indent
